WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.740
Welcome back.

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:05.240
In this class we will work through wall collision derivation, temperature and

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.520
rms speed, equipartition, heat capacities and mean free path.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.640
An ideal gas is a model of many tiny particles in random motion.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.040
Their own volume is negligible compared with the container, and intermolecular

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.440
forces are neglected except during collisions.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.400
Assume elastic wall collisions and an equilibrium gas with isotropic random

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.200
velocities and no overall flow.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.740
These assumptions make the derivation manageable, but they become poor

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.880
approximations in dense gases or near condensation.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.920
Use PV equals N Boltzmann constant T when counting molecules and PV equals nRT

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:41.920
when counting moles.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.480
They agree because N equals n Avogadro constant and R equals Avogadro constant

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.340
Boltzmann constant.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.542
Number density N/V has units of molecules per cubic metre; mass density rho

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:53.720
has

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.240
units of kilograms per cubic metre.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.720
A factor of Avogadro's number cannot be silently exchanged for a mass factor.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.440
Absolute temperature belongs in these equations; a Celsius reading must first

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.360
be converted to kelvin.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.460
Take a cube of side L and a molecule of mass m with x component of velocity

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.000
v_x.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.900
At an elastic collision with the wall normal to x, the component reverses.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.040
Momentum delivered to that wall has magnitude 2m|v_x|.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.060
The time between successive collisions with the same wall is

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.480
2L/|v_x|.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.180
Divide momentum transfer by time to get the average force contribution m v_x

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.380
squared/L; negative v_x gives the same squared contribution.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.240
Sum over N molecules and divide by wall area L squared.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.560
Pressure is m sum(v_x squared)/L cubed, or N m

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.700
average(v_x squared)/V.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.640
Isotropic equilibrium gives average(v_x squared) equals average(v_y squared)

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.320
equals average(v_z squared) equals average(v squared)/3.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:03.307
Therefore P equals N m average(v squared)/(3V) equals rho

00:02:03.307 --> 00:02:05.320
v_rms squared/3.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.720
The factor one third comes from three dimensional isotropy, not from an

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.140
arbitrary correction.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.020
This collision picture is an idealization; equilibrium averaging is what lets

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.220
irregular microscopic impacts produce a steady measured pressure.

00:02:19.056 --> 00:02:25.016
Combine the pressure result with PV equals N Boltzmann constant T.

00:02:25.676 --> 00:02:31.196
It gives m average(v squared) equals 3Boltzmann constant T,

00:02:31.936 --> 00:02:37.876
so mean translational kinetic energy per molecule is 3Boltzmann constant

00:02:37.876 --> 00:02:39.856
T/2.

00:02:39.856 --> 00:02:45.536
RMS speed is sqrt(3Boltzmann constant T/m), or

00:02:45.536 --> 00:02:51.456
sqrt(3RT/M) using molar mass M in kilograms

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:51.896
per mole.

00:02:53.076 --> 00:02:57.296
At the same temperature, different ideal gases have equal mean translational

00:02:57.296 --> 00:03:01.696
energy per molecule but different RMS speeds because their molecular masses

00:03:01.696 --> 00:03:02.056
differ.

00:03:02.976 --> 00:03:08.836
For nitrogen at 300 K, M equals 0.028 kilograms per mole and

00:03:08.836 --> 00:03:13.956
R equals 8.314 give about 517 metres per second.

00:03:13.956 --> 00:03:19.956
Helium with M equals 0.004 kilograms per mole is the square

00:03:19.956 --> 00:03:22.276
root of seven times faster at the same temperature.

00:03:23.556 --> 00:03:28.916
For the same gas, increasing temperature from 300 to 1200 K doubles RMS speed.

00:03:30.096 --> 00:03:34.396
An equilibrium gas has zero average vector velocity even though its average

00:03:34.396 --> 00:03:35.596
scalar speed is positive.

00:03:36.556 --> 00:03:41.486
RMS, mean and most probable speeds are different statistics; the RMS formula

00:03:41.486 --> 00:03:41.616
is

00:03:41.616 --> 00:03:42.956
not the value for every molecule.

00:03:43.896 --> 00:03:49.316
Classical equipartition assigns half Boltzmann constant T per molecule to each

00:03:49.316 --> 00:03:50.856
active quadratic energy term.

00:03:51.676 --> 00:03:55.016
With f active degrees of freedom, internal energy is f

00:03:55.016 --> 00:03:59.416
nRT/2 when f is constant.

00:04:00.496 --> 00:04:05.736
The molar heat capacity C V is fR/2.

00:04:05.736 --> 00:04:11.456
For an ideal gas, C P C V equals

00:04:11.456 --> 00:04:17.316
R, giving gamma equals (f+2)/f.

00:04:17.316 --> 00:04:22.416
A monatomic gas has three translational degrees and gamma equals 5/3.

00:04:22.416 --> 00:04:27.836
A rigid diatomic model with vibration inactive usually has five and gamma

00:04:27.836 --> 00:04:29.756
equals 7/5.

00:04:29.756 --> 00:04:34.236
A vibrational mode, when fully active classically, has both a kinetic and

00:04:34.796 --> 00:04:35.856
potential quadratic contribution.

00:04:37.036 --> 00:04:41.216
Active modes depend on temperature; quantum freezing of modes makes constant f

00:04:41.216 --> 00:04:44.416
an approximation rather than a universal molecular label.

00:04:45.436 --> 00:04:50.476
Two moles of monatomic ideal gas warmed by 10 K at fixed volume gain

00:04:50.476 --> 00:04:55.256
30R joules, about 249.42 J.

00:04:55.256 --> 00:04:59.236
Constant volume heat equals that gain because boundary work is zero.

00:05:00.176 --> 00:05:05.773
At constant pressure the same rise also requires expansion work, so heat uses

00:05:05.773 --> 00:05:05.846
C

00:05:05.846 --> 00:05:07.456
P instead.

00:05:07.656 --> 00:05:11.896
For a dilute equilibrium gas of identical hard sphere molecules with diameter

00:05:11.896 --> 00:05:16.956
d, the mean free path is 1/(the square root of two pi d squared

00:05:16.956 --> 00:05:17.356
number_density).

00:05:17.356 --> 00:05:21.636
The square root factor accounts for relative molecular motion.

00:05:22.456 --> 00:05:26.116
This is an average distance between collisions, not a fixed distance every

00:05:26.116 --> 00:05:26.956
particle must travel.

00:05:28.096 --> 00:05:31.796
Molecules per volume, not moles per volume or mass density, enter the formula

00:05:31.796 --> 00:05:32.256
directly.

00:05:32.876 --> 00:05:38.696
With d equals 3.0 times ten to the power of negative 10 m and number density

00:05:38.696 --> 00:05:44.536
2.5 times 10 squared5 per cubic metre, the mean free path is about 1.0

00:05:44.536 --> 00:05:47.096
times ten to the power of negative 7 m.

00:05:47.096 --> 00:05:51.000
At fixed temperature and diameter, doubling pressure doubles number density

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:51.156
and

00:05:51.156 --> 00:05:52.236
halves mean free path.

00:05:52.936 --> 00:05:57.476
At fixed pressure, increasing absolute temperature reduces number density and

00:05:57.476 --> 00:05:58.096
increases it.

00:05:58.096 --> 00:06:03.016
Separately, compression work against a constant external pressure is external

00:06:03.016 --> 00:06:08.436
pressure the volume change: compressing from 3 to 1 litres at 100000

00:06:08.436 --> 00:06:11.676
Pa gives 200 J work by the gas.

00:06:12.556 --> 00:06:17.116
Collision distance and macroscopic work are different quantities linked by a

00:06:17.116 --> 00:06:17.676
consistent gas model.
